1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,529
Chai Corner has six people now,
and the stall is busier than ever.

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:07,883
But one morning, Kabir's
cash counter breaks down.

3
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:11,353
Customers can't pay, so
the line stops moving.

4
00:00:11,653 --> 00:00:13,284
Asha's chai goes cold.

5
00:00:13,284 --> 00:00:15,467
Meena runs out of clean cups.

6
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:19,010
One small part fails, and
the whole stall suffers.

7
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:22,350
Chai Corner is not six separate jobs.

8
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:23,781
It is one system.

9
00:00:24,481 --> 00:00:26,576
So, what is the systems approach?

10
00:00:26,876 --> 00:00:33,793
It grew in the 1950s and 1960s, from
the ideas of Ludwig von Bertalanffy,

11
00:00:33,793 --> 00:00:36,026
an Austrian biologist.

12
00:00:36,376 --> 00:00:38,659
He studied living things as systems.

13
00:00:38,659 --> 00:00:41,434
In your body, every part needs the others.

14
00:00:41,784 --> 00:00:45,175
Management thinkers saw that
a business works the same way.

15
00:00:45,525 --> 00:00:50,489
The systems approach sees an organization
as one system of connected parts,

16
00:00:50,489 --> 00:00:52,305
working toward one goal.

17
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:55,046
Each part is called a subsystem.

18
00:00:55,046 --> 00:00:57,724
That's a smaller system
inside the big one.

19
00:00:58,074 --> 00:01:02,759
At Chai Corner, the kitchen, the cash
counter, and the cleaning are subsystems.

20
00:01:03,109 --> 00:01:05,082
They all depend on each other.

21
00:01:05,082 --> 00:01:08,157
That's why one broken
counter hurt the whole stall.

22
00:01:08,807 --> 00:01:11,587
Every system runs on the same simple loop.

23
00:01:11,937 --> 00:01:12,837
Inputs.

24
00:01:12,548 --> 00:01:15,781
What goes in, like milk,
tea leaves, and people.

25
00:01:16,131 --> 00:01:17,031
Process.

26
00:01:16,872 --> 00:01:18,949
This is called transformation.

27
00:01:18,949 --> 00:01:21,256
Asha turns the inputs into chai.

28
00:01:21,606 --> 00:01:22,506
Outputs.

29
00:01:22,355 --> 00:01:23,601
What comes out.

30
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:25,871
Hot chai, and happy customers.

31
00:01:26,221 --> 00:01:27,213
And feedback.

32
00:01:27,213 --> 00:01:31,718
Customers say the chai is too sweet,
so tomorrow Asha adds less sugar.

33
00:01:32,368 --> 00:01:34,701
Chai Corner is also an open system.

34
00:01:34,701 --> 00:01:37,786
That means it is linked
to the world around it.

35
00:01:38,136 --> 00:01:42,037
Customers, suppliers, the government,
and competitors all affect it.

36
00:01:42,387 --> 00:01:47,262
If milk prices go up, or a new stall
opens next door, Ravi has to adapt.

37
00:01:47,912 --> 00:01:49,191
One more big word.

38
00:01:49,191 --> 00:01:50,091
Synergy.

39
00:01:50,194 --> 00:01:53,495
It means the whole is greater
than the sum of its parts.

40
00:01:53,845 --> 00:01:57,135
Working alone, six people
serve sixty cups an hour.

41
00:01:57,135 --> 00:01:59,497
As one team, they serve a hundred.

42
00:02:00,147 --> 00:02:01,100
Quick check.

43
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:04,825
Ravi reads what customers say,
and changes tomorrow's menu.

44
00:02:04,825 --> 00:02:07,105
Which part of the system is this?

45
00:02:09,005 --> 00:02:09,905
Feedback!

46
00:02:09,767 --> 00:02:13,777
Information from the output comes
back and improves the system.

47
00:02:14,377 --> 00:02:15,389
So, remember.

48
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:20,758
An organization is one system, made
of connected parts called subsystems.

49
00:02:21,108 --> 00:02:25,567
Every system runs on a loop: inputs,
process, outputs, and feedback.

50
00:02:25,917 --> 00:02:30,229
It is open to the world around it, and
the whole is greater than its parts.

51
00:02:30,829 --> 00:02:35,212
Next time, we'll find out why there
is no one best way to manage, with

52
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:36,813
the contingency approach.
